    {
      "slug": "reddit-hoa-garden-gnome-bylaws",
      "script": "A garden gnome fine? Our HOA president left a $150 fine on my door for unapproved yard decor: one ceramic gnome my grandmother gave me. The bylaws never mention gnomes, so I read all forty pages that night. Petty? Maybe. Educational? Extremely. Half the street was violating something: unregistered trailers, a trampoline over the height limit, a shed built without a permit, and one parked boat sitting in her own driveway for three years. I filed one formal complaint per violation, cited every clause, and copied the whole board. The next meeting ran four hours. Suddenly, everyone cared deeply about setback rules. The board grandfathered in most violations, dropped my fine because no written decor policy existed, and the gnome stayed—holding a tiny approved sign. Who really won the HOA gnome war?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a four-word question hook, tightened the bylaw escalation, added one dry beat, and landed on the HOA gnome while preserving every shot phrase and number."
    },
    {
      "slug": "reddit-wrong-store-coupon",
      "script": "The coupon came from WHERE? A woman shoved a coupon across the counter, insisting our system was broken because it rejected her forty percent off code. She got loud, claimed she'd been a loyal customer for ten years, and demanded a manual override. Store policy lets me call the printed number on any coupon, so I did—right there on speaker. The automated line was not ours. It belonged to a rival chain two blocks over, cheerfully reading its own return policy. Tiny logo, enormous confidence. She'd grabbed the wrong flyer from her kitchen counter that morning. I offered our real weekly discount instead: ten percent, no drama. She accepted it silently, and the coupon stayed folded on the counter until the next customer used it as a napkin. Who won the rival-coupon showdown?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a five-word question hook, compressed the reveal, added one dry aside, and returned to the rival coupon while preserving every shot phrase and number."
    }
